    whyHeading: 'Why Electronics Brands Choose Packify',
    whyBody: 'Electronics brands choose Packify because the packaging protects the hardware and carries the unboxing without forcing a trade-off between them. Every build starts from the device: a die-cut foam, pulp, or corrugated bed seats the unit and its cables apart, reinforced corners and double-wall stock guard the parcel, and reserved panel space holds the model, voltage, and compatibility copy a technical buyer checks. Packify prints full CMYK with Pantone PMS matching, holds no MOQ, ships in 8-10 business days, and delivers free across Australia.',
    capabilities: [
      { title: 'Anti-Static Liner Options', desc: 'Anti-static interior liners are available to reduce electrostatic build-up around sensitive components through the distribution chain.' },
      { title: 'Custom Foam Engineering', desc: 'Precision foam inserts with millimeter-accuracy cutouts immobilize your device and accessories against movement damage.' },
      { title: 'Reinforced Construction', desc: 'Double-wall corrugated, reinforced corners, and fitted inserts built to carry electronics through postal and carrier handling.' },
      { title: 'Multi-Component Organization', desc: 'Complex accessory configurations, devices, cables, adapters, documentation, organized and presented systematically.' },
      { title: 'Premium Finish Program', desc: 'Soft-touch lamination, UV spot varnish, and metallic foiling create the premium exterior quality of leading technology brands.' },
      { title: 'Any Run Size', desc: 'No minimum on any electronics format, so a prototype run and a full production program print from the same retained dieline.' },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Do you offer anti-static electronics packaging?', a: 'Yes. Anti-static interior liners are available across box styles. You specify the liner your components require, and we build it into the insert.' },
      { q: 'Can you engineer custom foam inserts?', a: 'Yes. We produce custom foam inserts with precision cutouts for your specific device dimensions, working from your CAD files or physical samples.' },
      { q: 'How do you protect electronics in transit?', a: 'Through structure, not paperwork. Double-wall corrugated, reinforced corners, and a die-cut insert cut to your device hold the hardware still through postal and carrier handling.' },
      { q: 'What lead time should we plan for electronics packaging?', a: 'Production is 8-10 business days from artwork approval on every electronics order, including builds with custom foam or pulp inserts.' },
      { q: 'Can you produce premium rigid electronics packaging?', a: 'Yes. Wrapped rigid shells, magnetic closures, soft-touch lamination, spot UV, and metallic foil are available across the electronics range.' },
    ],
    heroSeoText: 'Custom electronics packaging from Packify spans rigid magnetic shells for earphones and smart watches, window tucks for chargers and cables, and cupped inserts for headphones. Every build seats the device and its accessories in a die-cut foam, pulp, or corrugated bed, prints full CMYK with Pantone PMS matching, and reserves panel space for the model, voltage, and compatibility copy a technical buyer reads. No MOQ, 8-10 business day production, and free Australia-wide shipping on every electronics packaging order.',
    learnMore: [
      { heading: 'Inserts Are the Whole Electronics Package', body: 'A device, its cables, and its adapter in one open cavity collide before the box reaches a shelf. Packify cuts a foam, molded pulp, or corrugated bed from the actual parts list so every component sits in its own recess. That interior geometry protects the hardware and stages the unboxing, which is why the insert, not the outer shell, is where an electronics package is won or lost.' },
      { heading: 'Protecting Electronics Through Structure', body: 'Packify guards electronics with construction, not paperwork. Double-wall corrugated stock, reinforced corners, and an insert cut to the device hold hardware still through postal and carrier handling. The board grade steps up with the shipped weight, so a light accessory and a heavy power bank each ride in a build specified for the load rather than a generic shipper.' },
      { heading: 'Window Tucks and Pegwall Electronics', body: 'Accessory buyers distrust a sealed box they cannot see into, and a wrong connector is the most common return in the category. A patched pane traced to the device lets a shopper confirm the fitting at the shelf, and a reinforced euro slot carries a coiled cable or a dense adapter on a pegwall. Reserved panel space keeps wattage, length, and compatibility figures scannable at arm length.' },
      { heading: 'Rigid Shells and the Unboxing Video', body: 'Technology buyers watch a product unbox before they own it, so the lid is part of the review. A wrapped rigid shell with a hinged spine and concealed magnets opens with a controlled click that reads as precision. Packify tunes magnet strength to lid weight and finishes the wrap in soft-touch, foil, or spot UV, so a mid-tier device presents with the tactile quality of a flagship.' },
      { heading: 'No Minimum for Hardware Launches', body: 'Hardware brands launch a single accessory, a limited color, or a pilot SKU long before volume justifies a large order. Packify sets no MOQ on any electronics format, so a first run prints at the quantity actually needed and a new connector variant tests without commitment. Retained dielines mean a product that sells scales into a full program on identical tooling and insert geometry.' },
    ],
